      <abstract>
        <para>
          Automating routine maintenance, such as regular backup scripts or
          routine tasks, is essential for effective Linux system
          administration. This article offers a comprehensive guide to &systemd;
          timers—a modern, flexible alternative to traditional task scheduling.
          It covers the full lifecycle of &systemd; timers, including creation,
          maintenance, testing and troubleshooting, as well as step-by-step
          guidance on migrating existing workflows from cron.
        </para>
        <para>
          <emphasis>Prerequisites:</emphasis>
        </para>
        <itemizedlist>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              Basic understanding of &systemd;.
            </para>
          </listitem>
          <listitem>
            <para>
              &rootuser; or sudo privileges. To use &systemd; timers as a
              regular user, refer to <xref linkend="systemd-timer-user"/> first.
            </para>
          </listitem>
        </itemizedlist>
      </abstract>
